1441504955,0.0,0.0,-3.576278118089249e-07,-1.6689160474925302e-05,-0.0017873755423352122,-2.145764938177308e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.00882392656058073,-5.125986263010418e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07,-1.4305104514278355e-06,-6.079655122448457e-06,-0.7380908727645874,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.4305104514278355e-06,-5.960462772236497e-07,-0.00015698630886618048,-8.344646857949556e-07,0.0,-2.3841830625315197e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-8.344646857949556e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-4.327203714638017e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.9073468138230965e-06,-4.302753448486328,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-3.755022044060752e-05,-0.001602795091457665,-0.04367946460843086,-0.04714401811361313,-0.014124192297458649,-4.0531076592742465e-06,-0.0017832106677815318,-8.070142939686775e-05,-2.861018856492592e-06,-9.536738616588991e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.005365258082747459,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-0.3553527891635895,-1.0728830375228426e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.0728830375228426e-06,-0.001110890181735158,-5.960462772236497e-07,-2.3841830625315197e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-0.00010990492592100054,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-6.079655122448457e-06,-3.576272320060525e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,678,6351,5109,400,89,3,1741,429,198,59,7265,90,6612,9,532,59,2359,91,1124,37018,90,89,481,220,19,15170,89,481,220,23,92,1124,1291,91,14581,220,16,11,90155,59,2359,91,1124,37018,90,89,481,220,16,17,15170,89,481,220,23,72,92,1124,1291,91,14581,1124,37018,90,20,15170,18,27275,59,408,90,6612,9,92,6269,678,279,9904,11,18663,553,76602,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind all complex numbers $z$ such that\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left| \\frac{z - 4}{z - 8} \\right| &= 1, \\\\\n\\left| \\frac{z - 12}{z - 8i} \\right| &= \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\end{align*}Enter all the solutions, separated by commas.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5618e45fbb00d0bfe32a1ab22abc3548de9cb2e55a52ab701bfcaafcf1d1ea4c"},"seconds":67.61753487586975,"seed":34647344257005201,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for all complex numbers $z$ that satisfy the given equations, we will break down the problem into manageable steps.\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the First Equation\n\nGiven the first equation:\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 4}{z - 8} \\right| = 1\n\\]\n\nThis implies that the ratio of distances from \\(z\\) to 4 and from \\(z\\) to 8 is 1. In geometric terms, \\(z\\) must lie on the perpendicular bisector of the line segment joining 4 and 8 on the complex plane.\n\nThe midpoint of the segment joining 4 and 8 is:\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint} = \\frac{4 + 8}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector is a vertical line through this midpoint. Hence, \\(z\\) must be of the form \\(6 + bi\\) for some real number \\(b\\).\n\n### Step 2: Analyze the Second Equation\n\nGiven the second equation:\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 12}{z - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(z = 6 + bi\\). Substitute this form into the equation:\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{(6 + bi) - 12}{(6 + bi) - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{-6 + bi}{6 + (b - 8)i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the Magnitude\n\nWe need to find the magnitudes of the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\left| -6 + bi \\right| = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + b^2} = \\sqrt{36 + b^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left| 6 + (b - 8)i \\right| = \\sqrt{6^2 + (b - 8)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + (b - 8)^2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{36 + b^2}}{\\sqrt{36 + (b - 8)^2}} = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\nSquare both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\\[\n\\frac{36 + b^2}{36 + (b - 8)^2} = \\frac{25}{9}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiply to solve for \\(b\\):\n\\[\n9(36 + b^2) = 25(36 + (b - 8)^2)\n\\]\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 900 + 25(b^2 - 16b + 64)\n\\]\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 900 + 25b^2 - 400b + 1600\n\\]\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 2500 + 25b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\nRearrange to form a quadratic equation:\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 2500 + 25b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\\[\n0 = 2500 - 324 + 25b^2 - 9b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\\[\n0 = 2176 + 16b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\\[\n16b^2 - 400b + 2176 = 0\n\\]\n\nDivide the entire equation by 16:\n\\[\nb^2 - 25b + 136 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{25 \\pm \\sqrt{625 - 544}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{25 \\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{25 \\pm 9}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\(b\\) are:\n\\[\nb = \\frac{34}{2} = 17 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad b = \\frac{16}{2} = 8\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Form the Complex Numbers\n\nThe corresponding complex numbers \\(z\\) are:\n\\[\nz = 6 + 17i \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z = 6 + 8i\n\\]\n\nHence, the solutions are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6 + 17i, 6 + 8i}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6 + 17i, 6 + 8i}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for all complex numbers $z$ that satisfy the given equations, we will break down the problem into manageable steps.\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the First Equation\n\nGiven the first equation:\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 4}{z - 8} \\right| = 1\n\\]\n\nThis implies that the ratio of distances from \\(z\\) to 4 and from \\(z\\) to 8 is 1. In geometric terms, \\(z\\) must lie on the perpendicular bisector of the line segment joining 4 and 8 on the complex plane.\n\nThe midpoint of the segment joining 4 and 8 is:\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint} = \\frac{4 + 8}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector is a vertical line through this midpoint. Hence, \\(z\\) must be of the form \\(6 + bi\\) for some real number \\(b\\).\n\n### Step 2: Analyze the Second Equation\n\nGiven the second equation:\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 12}{z - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(z = 6 + bi\\). Substitute this form into the equation:\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{(6 + bi) - 12}{(6 + bi) - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{-6 + bi}{6 + (b - 8)i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the Magnitude\n\nWe need to find the magnitudes of the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\left| -6 + bi \\right| = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + b^2} = \\sqrt{36 + b^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left| 6 + (b - 8)i \\right| = \\sqrt{6^2 + (b - 8)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + (b - 8)^2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{36 + b^2}}{\\sqrt{36 + (b - 8)^2}} = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\nSquare both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\\[\n\\frac{36 + b^2}{36 + (b - 8)^2} = \\frac{25}{9}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiply to solve for \\(b\\):\n\\[\n9(36 + b^2) = 25(36 + (b - 8)^2)\n\\]\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 900 + 25(b^2 - 16b + 64)\n\\]\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 900 + 25b^2 - 400b + 1600\n\\]\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 2500 + 25b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\nRearrange to form a quadratic equation:\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 2500 + 25b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\\[\n0 = 2500 - 324 + 25b^2 - 9b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\\[\n0 = 2176 + 16b^2 - 400b\n\\]\n\\[\n16b^2 - 400b + 2176 = 0\n\\]\n\nDivide the entire equation by 16:\n\\[\nb^2 - 25b + 136 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{25 \\pm \\sqrt{625 - 544}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{25 \\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{25 \\pm 9}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\(b\\) are:\n\\[\nb = \\frac{34}{2} = 17 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad b = \\frac{16}{2} = 8\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Form the Complex Numbers\n\nThe corresponding complex numbers \\(z\\) are:\n\\[\nz = 6 + 17i \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z = 6 + 8i\n\\]\n\nHence, the solutions are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6 + 17i, 6 + 8i}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:888"}
